<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Galicia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as New Galicia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> was the name of the Polish lands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a> and Radom, that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> annexed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a> in 1795. Austria lost the province by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a> in 1809 to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, regaining only the Kraków region later, in 1846.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-galicia.jpg" alt="West Galicia" width="320" height="313" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kpalion · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Galicia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
